## v2: polite httpx scraper (600 requests, /listings/1..600)

{
  "ts": "2026-11-19T11:41:46.413560+00:00",
  "ip": "127.0.0.1",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/listings/1",
  "query": "",
  "status": 200,
  "response_ms": 2.9,
  "http_version": "1.1",
  "user_agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.15; rv:158.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/158.0",
  "accept_language": "es-ES,es;q=0.9,en;q=0.8",
  "referer": "http://localhost:8000/search?city=Madrid",
  "has_cookie": false,
  "sec_fetch_dest": null,
  "sec_fetch_mode": null,
  "header_order": [
    "host",
    "accept",
    "accept-encoding",
    "connection",
    "user-agent",
    "accept-language",
    "referer"
  ]
}

- user_agent: now a copy of a real Firefox string. The format is correct,
  so the UA alone no longer gives it away. Cost to fake: one line.
- accept_language "es-ES,es;q=0.9,en;q=0.8": added by hand. It looks
  plausible, so it stops being a signal on its own.
- referer: added by hand. It points to /search?city=Madrid, a page this
  client never requested. Every one of the 600 rows carries the same value.
- header_order: host, accept, accept-encoding, connection, user-agent,
  accept-language, referer. user-agent is fifth (Firefox puts it second),
  and the headers the attacker added sit at the end, because the library
  appends them after its own defaults. 7 headers instead of 14.
- sec_fetch_dest null, sec_fetch_mode null: Firefox sends both on every
  request. This is still the cleanest tell in the row.

---
running the analyzer:
client                          n  navs  assets   gap_ms    cv  seq%  lang%  noref%  fav  burst  verdict
Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; In    600   600       0      156  0.38   100    100       0    0     70  ok

- burst 70 (threshold 100): the random 50-250 ms pauses keep the rate
  under the limit, so the burst rule does not fire. With a threshold of
  50 it would have been caught, which shows the threshold is a trade-off
  and not a fact.
- gap_ms 156: about 6 requests per second. Slower than v1 (1 ms), still
  far faster than a person reading listings.
- cv 0.38: this is the random generator showing through, not behavior.
  A uniform pause of 0.05 to 0.25 s gives
  0.2 / (sqrt(12) * 0.15) = about 0.385. human_sim (0.43) lands in the
  same zone for the same reason, so cv does not separate them yet.
  Compare with the real Firefox before choosing a threshold.
- seq% 100: unchanged from v1. IDs go 1, 2, 3 with no gaps. The attacker
  fixed the headers and the speed, but not the order it walks the site.
- lang% 100, noref% 0: both columns now look human, and both were
  defeated by adding one header. The cheapest signals are the first
  ones an attacker removes.
- fav 0: still no favicon, because it is not a browser.
- assets 0: not evidence by itself. The analyzer counts rows with no
  sec_fetch_dest as navigations, so httpx rows always land in "navs".
- verdict ok: this is wrong in the sense that matters. The row contradicts
  the Firefox claim (header order, missing sec-fetch headers), but no rule
  reads those yet. The verdict only reflects the burst rule.

What v2 teaches
- Changed (cheap to fake): UA string, accept-language, referer, pause length.
- Left alone (set by the library or the habit): header order, number of
  headers, sec-fetch-* headers, sequential IDs, no favicon.
- The referer is consistent on its face and inconsistent in context: one
  value repeated 600 times, pointing to a page the client never visited.
- Next rules to write: header_mismatch (UA claim vs header order and
  sec-fetch), and top_referer_share (one referer repeated again and again).